{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>1,025 Built-In Cut Charts Across 3 Torch Families, CSV Interchange &amp; Feature Toggling</h2>
          <p>
            Wrong feedrate, arc voltage, pierce delay, or kerf turns good tube into scrap. You get <strong>1,025 built-in cut presets across 3 plasma torch families</strong> ready to run: <strong>Hypertherm Powermax SYNC (<code class="mono">3.2 mm / 0.125"</code> standoff, 564 presets)</strong>, <strong>Hypertherm Duramax / Legacy 30XP–105 (<code class="mono">1.5 mm / 0.060"</code> standoff, 269 presets)</strong>, and <strong>Generic Air Plasma (<code class="mono">1.5 mm / 0.060"</code> standoff, 192 presets)</strong> tuned for Razorweld 45, PrimeWeld Cut60, Everlast PowerPlasma, and Thermal Dynamics Cutmaster. Charts cover Standard Best Quality, Standard High Prod, FineCut High Speed, and FineCut Low Speed in Metric (<code class="mono">mm, mm/min, bar</code>) and Imperial (<code class="mono">GA / in, IPM, PSI</code>) for Mild Steel, Stainless Steel, and Aluminum.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-top: 0.85rem;">
            Drop a 3D STEP tube or flat DXF/SVG file onto the table to pair your wall thickness or plate gauge with your active torch family. Cut speed, arc voltage, pierce height, air pressure, and standoff height (<code class="mono">1.5 mm</code> vs <code class="mono">3.2 mm</code>) load automatically. Edit any parameter inline, add custom shop gauges, or share tables across machines with <strong>unit-aware CSV Import &amp; Export (<code class="mono">⬇ Export CSV</code> / <code class="mono">⬆ Import CSV</code>)</strong>. Need to skip a bolt hole or slot without editing CAD? Uncheck it in the feature table to update your 3D toolpath, pierce count, and G-code on the fly while sever cuts stay locked.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Factory Presets</span>
              <span class="value">1,025 Charts (3 Torch Families)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">CSV Interchange</span>
              <span class="value">Unit-Aware CSV Import/Export</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Thickness Pairing</span>
              <span class="value">Automatic 3D CAD Wall Detection</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Selective Cutting</span>
              <span class="value">Per-Contour Omission &amp; Safety Locks</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Open the Cut Charts Manager (<code class="mono">🔥 Cut Charts</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Open the Cut Charts Manager from any view: 3D assembly, single tube inspection, or stick nesting.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Check the top-right header bar next to the <code class="mono">ⓘ About</code> button.</li>
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip amber">🔥 Cut Charts</span>.</li>
              <li>Browse factory presets, search gauges, and edit custom shop charts without unloading your CAD part or resetting toolpaths.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1: Clicking '🔥 Cut Charts' in the top-right header bar</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-charts-1-open.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-charts-1-open.png" alt="Step 1: Clicking Cut Charts button in top-right application header" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Browse &amp; Filter 1,025 Factory Presets Across 3 Plasma Torch Families</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Filter <strong>1,025 factory cut presets</strong> across 3 plasma torch families to match your torch standoff, voltage curve, and consumables:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Hypertherm Powermax SYNC (<code class="mono">3.2 mm / 0.125"</code> standoff, 564 presets):</strong> For Powermax 45/65/85/105 SYNC torches running single-piece cartridges (<code class="mono">810500MU</code> Rev 4, <code class="mono">45A–105A</code>). Covers Standard Best Quality, Standard High Prod, FineCut High Speed, and FineCut Low Speed in Metric (<code class="mono">mm, mm/min, bar</code>) and Imperial (<code class="mono">GA / in, IPM, PSI</code>). Default family on SimCNC profiles.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Hypertherm Duramax / Legacy 30XP–105 (<code class="mono">1.5 mm / 0.060"</code> standoff, 269 presets):</strong> For Powermax30 XP, 45 XP, 65, 85, and 105 torches running mechanized shielded consumables (<code class="mono">30A, 45A, 65A, 85A, 105A</code> + <code class="mono">45A FineCut</code>, ~<code class="mono">114–138 V</code> arc voltage). Default on LinuxCNC QTPlasmaC, Mach4, MASSO G3, Mach3, Centroid Acorn, UCCNC, FlashCut, EdingCNC, PlanetCNC, and ShopSabre WinCNC.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Generic Air Plasma (<code class="mono">1.5 mm / 0.060"</code> standoff, 192 presets):</strong> Conservative blowback / non-HF air-plasma charts (<code class="mono">30A, 45A, 60A, 80A</code>, ~<code class="mono">106–128 V</code>, <code class="mono">72 PSI / 5.0 bar</code>) tuned for <strong>Razorweld 45, PrimeWeld Cut60, Everlast PowerPlasma, and Thermal Dynamics Cutmaster</strong>. Default on Langmuir FireControl and grblHAL.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Material &amp; Amperage Filters:</strong> Filter by <code class="mono">Mild Steel</code>, <code class="mono">Stainless Steel</code>, or <code class="mono">Aluminum</code>. The amperage dropdown updates to match your torch's real range (<code class="mono">[30, 45, 60, 80]</code> for Generic Air Plasma, <code class="mono">[30, 45, 65, 85, 105]</code> for Duramax, and <code class="mono">[45, 65, 85, 105]</code> for SYNC).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Machine Standoff Lock:</strong> Pick your torch family in your machine profile to lock in its cut height (<code class="mono">1.5 mm / 0.060"</code> vs <code class="mono">3.2 mm / 0.125"</code>) and arc voltage curve. Your torch stays off the tube and out of the air.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2: Browsing and filtering 1,025 factory cut charts across 3 plasma torch families</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-charts-2-modal.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-charts-2-modal.png" alt="Step 2: Browsing and filtering 1,025 cut charts across Hypertherm SYNC, Duramax, and Generic Air Plasma" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Edit Parameters Inline or Add Custom Shop Presets</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Every plasma table cuts a little differently based on air dryness, ground clamp placement, and gantry stiffness. Tune any preset or save your own shop charts:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Inline Editing:</strong> Click any cell in a chart row (<code class="mono">Cut Speed</code>, <code class="mono">Kerf Width</code>, <code class="mono">Arc Voltage</code>, or <code class="mono">Pierce Delay</code>), type your shop value, and click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Save</span>.</li>
              <li><strong>Add Custom Presets:</strong> Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">+ Add Preset</span> in the top-right header of the modal to save charts for non-standard tube walls, 4130 chromoly, Hardox, or custom consumables.</li>
              <li><strong>Saved Across Restarts:</strong> Your edited rows and custom charts stay saved locally across browser refreshes and app restarts.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 3: Editing cut chart row parameters inline or creating custom shop presets</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-charts-3-edit.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-charts-3-edit.png" alt="Step 3: Editing cut parameters inline or clicking Add Preset" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Import &amp; Export Cut Charts via CSV (<code class="mono">⬇ Export CSV</code> / <code class="mono">⬆ Import CSV</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Bring in your torch maker's spreadsheet, back up tuned shop speeds, or share charts across tables with <strong>unit-aware CSV Export and Import</strong>:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Unit-Aware Export (<code class="mono">⬇ Export CSV</code>):</strong> Click <span class="ui-chip">⬇ Export CSV</span> to download your active table (or filtered view) in your machine's unit system:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong>Metric (<code class="mono">mm / G21</code>):</strong> Exports <code class="mono">thickness_mm, feed_mm_min, kerf_mm, cut_height_mm, pierce_height_mm, pierce_time_s, cut_voltage, gas_pressure_bar</code> plus optional center-mark and peck columns.</li>
                  <li><strong>Imperial (<code class="mono">in / G20</code>):</strong> Exports <code class="mono">thickness_in, feed_ipm, kerf_in, cut_height_in, pierce_height_in, pierce_time_s, cut_voltage, gas_pressure_psi</code> plus optional center-mark and peck columns.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Auto-Detected Unit Headers (<code class="mono">⬆ Import CSV</code>):</strong> Drop in your vendor or Excel CSV. Column headers (<code class="mono">_in</code> / <code class="mono">_ipm</code> / <code class="mono">_psi</code> vs <code class="mono">_mm</code> / <code class="mono">_mm_min</code> / <code class="mono">_bar</code>) are detected automatically, so importing an Imperial IPM chart into a Metric workspace converts every speed, kerf, and height cleanly.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Safe Merge on Import:</strong> Rows matching an existing <code class="mono">(name, source, material, amperage, consumable_type)</code> update in place. New rows append without wiping factory presets, and any new <code class="mono">source</code> name (like <code class="mono">Razorweld 45 Shop</code> or <code class="mono">Fab-Table 2 Tuned</code>) shows up immediately in the Source dropdown.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout info" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">📋 Sample CSV Headers &amp; Row Format</div>
              <p>
                Edit in Excel, Google Sheets, or any text editor using standard Metric or Imperial headers:
              </p>
              <pre><code class="language-csv"># Metric Example:
name,source,material,amperage,consumable_type,thickness_mm,feed_mm_min,kerf_mm,cut_height_mm,pierce_height_mm,pierce_time_s,cut_voltage,gas_pressure_bar
1.5mm Mild Steel 45A,Shop Tuned,Mild Steel,45,Standard,1.5,4200,1.15,1.5,3.8,0.2,118,5.0

# Imperial Example:
name,source,material,amperage,consumable_type,thickness_in,feed_ipm,kerf_in,cut_height_in,pierce_height_in,pierce_time_s,cut_voltage,gas_pressure_psi
16 GA Mild Steel 45A,Shop Tuned,Mild Steel,45,Standard,0.0598,165,0.045,0.060,0.150,0.2,118,72.5</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Inspect Automatic Wall-Thickness Matching &amp; Standoff Binding</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Load a STEP file, and your tube's nominal wall thickness (for example, <code class="mono">3.56 mm / 1/8"</code>) is measured right from the inner and outer walls.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Look under <strong>Cross-Section &amp; Dimensions</strong> in the left sidebar for the <span class="ui-chip amber">3.56mm / 1/8"</span> wall thickness badge.</li>
              <li>The matching cut chart for your machine's torch family loads automatically: <strong>Feedrate</strong> (<code class="mono">2850 mm/min</code>), <strong>Kerf Compensation</strong> (<code class="mono">1.25 mm</code>), <strong>Arc Voltage</strong> (<code class="mono">128 V</code>), and <strong>Pierce Delay</strong> (<code class="mono">0.4 s</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>Built-In <code class="mono">1/8&Prime;</code> (<code class="mono">3.175 mm</code>) Presets &amp; In-Between Gauges:</strong> Standard <code class="mono">1/8&Prime;</code> (<code class="mono">3.175 mm</code>) Mild Steel and Stainless Steel structural tube and plate have dedicated factory charts. If your wall falls between standard gauges (like <code class="mono">3.0 mm</code> between <code class="mono">12 GA</code> and <code class="mono">1/8&Prime;</code>, or <code class="mono">5.0 mm</code> between <code class="mono">3/16&Prime;</code> and <code class="mono">1/4&Prime;</code>), feedrate, voltage, and pierce delay blend smoothly between the nearest charts. You get a clean cut with zero hand math at the table.</li>
              <li><strong>Manual Override:</strong> Want to cut 1/8" tube with 45A FineCut instead of 65A standard? Pick any chart from the dropdown, or click reset to jump back to the auto-matched preset.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 5: Automatic wall-thickness matching badge and cut parameters in the left sidebar</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-charts-4-session.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-charts-4-session.png" alt="Step 5: Automatic wall thickness matching badge and cut parameters in sidebar" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 6
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-6">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 06</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Toggle Contours in Single Part Inspection</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Skip hand-drilled bolt holes, optional slots, or fixture marks without waiting on CAD edits.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Scroll to the feature table below the 3D viewport in Single Part Inspection to see each cutout's type, axial position, and size.</li>
              <li><strong>Enable/Disable Checkboxes:</strong> Uncheck any <code class="mono">HOLE</code>, <code class="mono">SLOT</code>, <code class="mono">CUTOUT</code>, or <code class="mono">SCRIBE</code> box on the left to drop that cut from your job.</li>
              <li><strong>Cut, Plasma Mark &amp; Scribe Modes (<code class="mono">⚡ Cut</code> / <code class="mono">🔥 Plasma Mark</code> / <code class="mono">✏️ Scribe</code>):</strong> Switch any contour (or the whole part via <code class="mono">✏️ Scribe All</code>) between <strong>⚡ Cut</strong> (<code class="mono">#1114=1</code>), <strong>🔥 Plasma Mark</strong> (<code class="mono">#1114=2</code>, <code class="mono">15A</code> target or cartridge minimum, <code class="mono">0s</code> pierce delay, exact CAD line without swapping consumables), and <strong>✏️ Scribe</strong> (<code class="mono">#1114=3</code> strictly for Coaxial EasyScribe with <code class="mono">-3 mm</code> spring compression, arc OFF, and mandatory <code class="mono">M0</code> swap pause, or <code class="mono">G52</code> + <code class="mono">M8</code>/<code class="mono">M9</code> for SimCNC pneumatic offset air-scribes). Center-point spotting also supports pneumatic peck center-spotting across all 13 third-party controllers. Mechanical scribes run first, then plasma marks, then through-cuts.</li>
              <li><strong>One-Click Auto-Scribe Part IDs &amp; Witness Marks:</strong> Load a multi-part STEP assembly, and each tube gets a short piece ID (<code class="mono">P1</code>, <code class="mono">P2</code>, &hellip;, <code class="mono">P12</code>). Turn on <strong>Auto-Scribe Part IDs</strong> to stencil stick-font labels on the longest clear flat face or top tube crest, clearing holes, miters, and sidecar art with collinear-merged high-speed witness marking and clean <code class="mono">M5</code> &rarr; <code class="mono">G0</code> &rarr; <code class="mono">M3 S1</code> hops via EasyScribe (<code class="mono">#1114=3</code>), pneumatic air-scribe, or Plasma Mark (<code class="mono">#1114=2</code> at <code class="mono">#1111=15A</code>). When branch tubes or brackets meet a header (<code class="mono">&le; 0.5 mm</code> gap), weld witness footprints and mating IDs scribe right at each coped end for instant fit-up.</li>
              <li><strong>Locked Sever Cuts:</strong> Tube cutoff cuts (<code class="mono">STARTCUT</code> and <code class="mono">ENDCUT</code>) stay locked with a badge so you never accidentally leave a part attached to the stick.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 6: Feature table checkboxes for internal cutouts with locked cutoff cuts</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-charts-5-checkbox.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-charts-5-checkbox.png" alt="Step 6: Interactive enable/disable checkboxes for tube cutouts with locked sever cuts" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 7
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-7">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 07</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Verify Omitted Toolpaths &amp; Live G-Code</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Uncheck a contour, and your preview, pierce count, and G-code update right away:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>3D Toolpath:</strong> The omitted cutout, lead-in, and lead-out vanish from the 3D preview immediately.</li>
              <li><strong>Pierce Count &amp; Cycle Time:</strong> Your pierce counter drops right away (for example, from 5 to 4 pierces) and cycle time recalculates, saving air, nozzles, and run time.</li>
              <li><strong>Live G-Code:</strong> The G-code panel on the right strips out the plunge (<code class="mono">G00</code>), torch fire (<code class="mono">M03</code>), pierce delay (<code class="mono">G04</code>), cut move (<code class="mono">G01</code>), and arc off (<code class="mono">M05</code>) for that contour automatically.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 7: Omitted feature removed from 3D toolpath, pierce count reduced, and G-code updated</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-charts-6-omitted.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-charts-6-omitted.png" alt="Step 7: Omitted feature excluded from 3D cut path and live G-code recompiled" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
